  "worked_solution": "Let $r$ be the radius of the snowball and let $A$ be its surface area. Both $r$ and $A$ change with time. In particular, we are given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{1}{5}\\,\\text{in/s},\n$$\n\nand we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ when $A=100\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{2}.$\n\nTo start, let's write down the formula for the surface area of a sphere and then differentiate it using implicit differentiation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =4\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}r}(4\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 8\\pi r\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then calculate the value of $r$ using $A=100\\pi .$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =4\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ 100\\pi  & =4\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ 25 & ={r}^{2} \\\\ r & =\\pm 5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince radius cannot be negative, we have $r=5.$\n\nWe now substitute $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{1}{5}$ and $r=5$ into our expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot 8\\pi \\cdot 5 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =-8\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the surface area of the snowball is decreasing at a rate of $8\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{2}\\text{/s}.$",
